And a lot of people don't realize just how important business English really is.

9
00:00:35,090 --> 00:00:35,670
Yeah, that's true.

10
00:00:36,110 --> 00:00:40,430
It's easy to just think of it as, you know, good English, but it's way more than that.

11
00:00:40,500 --> 00:00:40,780
Right.

12
00:00:41,220 --> 00:00:41,740
It really is.

13
00:00:42,120 --> 00:00:46,530
You know, in today's global business world, business English, it's like a superpower.

14
00:00:46,830 --> 00:00:55,110
Studies actually show that there's a strong link between how well you know English and how much money you can potentially earn.

15
00:00:55,230 --> 00:01:02,320
It can open doors to collaborating with people from all over and really push your career forward, especially if you work in multinational companies.

16
00:01:02,300 --> 00:01:02,780
Wow.

17
00:01:02,920 --> 00:01:05,120
OK, that is that's pretty convincing.

18
00:01:05,440 --> 00:01:06,850
So it's not just about sounding smart.

19
00:01:06,870 --> 00:01:08,950
It's about like unlocking opportunities.

20
00:01:09,490 --> 00:01:13,600
What exactly makes business English so different from just casual conversation?

21
00:01:13,640 --> 00:01:14,720
What makes it so special?

22
00:01:14,920 --> 00:01:20,810
Well, it all boils down to the level of, well, you know, precision, clarity and formality.

23
00:01:21,730 --> 00:01:24,180
These are expected in a professional environment.

24
00:01:24,540 --> 00:01:25,140
Think of it this way.

25
00:01:25,640 --> 00:01:29,400
Every single word you use in a business context, it carries weight.

26
00:01:29,380 --> 00:01:32,790
You know, misunderstandings, those can lead to costly mistakes.

27
00:01:33,550 --> 00:01:42,640
Business English basically gives you the skills to use language strategically, ensuring your message is understood correctly and effectively.

28
00:01:42,910 --> 00:01:43,110
I see.

29
00:01:43,120 --> 00:01:44,990
So it's more than just grammar and vocabulary.

30
00:01:45,010 --> 00:01:48,470
It's about like using language as a tool to actually get things done.

31
00:01:48,540 --> 00:01:49,160
Exactly.

32
00:01:49,420 --> 00:01:52,270
And to really do that effectively, you've got to master some key areas.

33
00:01:52,310 --> 00:01:54,700
So there's vocabulary, writing, speaking, listening.

34
00:01:55,080 --> 00:01:57,770
And this is really crucial, cultural etiquette.

35
00:01:57,790 --> 00:01:58,130
Oh, yeah.

36
00:01:58,170 --> 00:01:58,870
Cultural etiquette.

37
00:01:58,890 --> 00:02:00,130
That can be...

38
00:02:00,330 --> 00:02:00,990
A bit tricky.

39
00:02:01,010 --> 00:02:05,680
You hear stories sometimes about, you know, business deals falling apart because someone gave like a thumbs up in the wrong country.

40
00:02:05,840 --> 00:02:06,400
Oh, it's true.

41
00:02:06,460 --> 00:02:09,560
Those cultural differences, they can make or break a deal.

42
00:02:09,630 --> 00:02:16,410
What might be considered like polite or friendly in one culture could actually be disrespectful somewhere else.

43
00:02:16,790 --> 00:02:20,220
We'll definitely dive into those complexities of cultural etiquette a bit later.

44
00:02:20,600 --> 00:02:21,680
Okay, I'm hooked.

45
00:02:22,520 --> 00:02:24,930
I'm ready to start building my business English toolkit.

46
00:02:26,350 --> 00:02:27,270
What's the first step?

47
00:02:27,350 --> 00:02:28,550
Where do we even begin?

48
00:02:28,840 --> 00:02:30,020
Well, let's start with vocabulary.

49
00:02:30,040 --> 00:02:32,080
It's like the building blocks of good communication.

50
00:02:32,100 --> 00:02:34,140
OK, word power.

51
00:02:34,180 --> 00:02:36,550
But how many business terms do I really need to know?

52
00:02:37,030 --> 00:02:39,210
I don't need to memorize the entire dictionary.

53
00:02:39,370 --> 00:02:41,390
No, you don't need to be a walking dictionary.

54
00:02:41,450 --> 00:02:47,000
But having a solid grasp of terminology specific to your industry, that's essential.

55
00:02:47,120 --> 00:02:53,920
It shows that you understand the language of your field and that you can actually contribute meaningfully to conversations.

56
00:02:53,980 --> 00:02:59,850
Like if you're in finance, you need to understand terms like ROI or return on investment.

57
00:02:59,950 --> 00:03:00,210
Right.

58
00:03:00,310 --> 00:03:01,150
Yeah, that makes sense.

59
00:03:01,170 --> 00:03:03,210
It's like speaking the secret language of finance.

60
00:03:03,290 --> 00:03:03,850
Exactly.

61
00:03:04,010 --> 00:03:08,700
And the good news is you don't need to like use flashcards for every new term.

62
00:03:09,040 --> 00:03:12,970
Instead, just try immersing yourself in content that's specific to your industry.

63
00:03:13,350 --> 00:03:16,420
You know, read journals, blogs or articles related to your field.

64
00:03:16,720 --> 00:03:19,910
Pay attention to how those terms are actually used in context.

65
00:03:19,930 --> 00:03:22,870
This will help you absorb the language more naturally.

66
00:03:22,850 --> 00:03:28,220
OK, so it's about understanding the idea behind the terms, not just memorizing definitions.

67
00:03:28,260 --> 00:03:29,520
That sounds way easier.

68
00:03:29,660 --> 00:03:30,150
Absolutely.

69
00:03:30,250 --> 00:03:40,620
And as you read, just keep a like running list of those new words and phrases, jot down the definitions and even create example sentences, you know, to help solidify your understanding.

70
00:03:40,600 --> 00:03:48,720
Okay, I can already see myself with my business English vocabulary journal, like my secret weapon to decoding the language of my industry.

71
00:03:49,060 --> 00:03:49,420
Yeah.

72
00:03:49,440 --> 00:03:50,320
But what about writing?

73
00:03:50,660 --> 00:03:53,240
Sometimes just writing a simple email feels impossible.

74
00:03:53,360 --> 00:03:54,650
Oh, I totally get that feeling.

75
00:03:55,050 --> 00:03:57,650
Writing in a professional context, yeah, can be daunting.

76
00:03:58,110 --> 00:04:00,070
But here's a tip that can save you time and stress.

77
00:04:00,570 --> 00:04:01,060
Templates.

78
00:04:01,480 --> 00:04:01,780
Use them.

79
00:04:02,100 --> 00:04:02,760
Templates?

80
00:04:03,180 --> 00:04:04,840
Don't those sound a bit, you know, generic?

81
00:04:04,880 --> 00:04:08,050
How can I make sure my writing still sounds authentic, like my own?

82
00:04:08,030 --> 00:04:08,790
That's a great question.

83
00:04:08,850 --> 00:04:12,670
So think of templates as a framework, not like a rigid formula.

84
00:04:12,710 --> 00:04:18,540
They give you a starting point for emails and reports, making sure you include all the essential stuff clearly and concisely.

85
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:21,830
From there, you can personalize it with your own voice and style.

86
00:04:22,030 --> 00:04:24,970
So it's about using templates as a foundation and then...

87
00:04:24,950 --> 00:04:27,660
Like building on that with my own creativity, what I want to say.

88
00:04:27,680 --> 00:04:28,260
Exactly.

89
00:04:28,300 --> 00:04:34,250
And to really make your writing shine, consider using tools like, you know, Grammarly or Hemingway Editor.

90
00:04:34,270 --> 00:04:40,900
They can help you catch those pesky grammar errors and even give you suggestions on how to make your writing clearer and stronger.

91
00:04:41,030 --> 00:04:41,510
Awesome.

92
00:04:41,530 --> 00:04:42,780
Technology to the rescue.

93
00:04:42,940 --> 00:04:43,980
That takes some of the pressure off.

94
00:04:44,740 --> 00:04:47,180
But writing is just one part of the whole picture.

95
00:04:47,680 --> 00:04:48,520
What about speaking?

96
00:04:49,440 --> 00:04:51,310
Honestly, public speaking freaks me out.

97
00:04:51,330 --> 00:04:53,370
Oh, public speaking is a super common fear.

98
00:04:54,090 --> 00:04:56,740
But there are strategies you can use to overcome it.

99
00:04:56,780 --> 00:04:59,180
Have you ever heard of the feel method?

100
00:04:59,200 --> 00:05:00,360
No.

101
00:05:01,020 --> 00:05:01,520
Tell me about it.

102
00:05:01,720 --> 00:05:05,490
So it stands for focus, envision, engage, and let go.

103
00:05:05,510 --> 00:05:10,890
So first, you focus on your main message, like the one key takeaway you want your audience to remember.

104
00:05:11,440 --> 00:05:14,240
Then envision yourself delivering that message and doing it well.

105
00:05:14,580 --> 00:05:18,170
Engage with your audience, make eye contact, use natural gestures.

106
00:05:18,810 --> 00:05:23,390
And finally, let go of that fear of messing up and just trust that you've prepared.

107
00:05:23,470 --> 00:05:24,310
That's really helpful.

108
00:05:24,540 --> 00:05:26,900
It breaks the whole process down into smaller steps.

109
00:05:27,660 --> 00:05:32,840
Are there any other ways to practice speaking in a business context besides public speaking?

110
00:05:33,140 --> 00:05:33,640
Absolutely.

111
00:05:33,930 --> 00:05:40,090
Practicing different scenarios like mock interviews or presentations with a friend, that can be super helpful.

112
00:05:40,170 --> 00:05:46,580
It gives you a safe space to get comfortable with the flow of business conversations and to get some feedback.

113
00:05:46,710 --> 00:05:47,110
Oh, yeah.

114
00:05:47,210 --> 00:05:47,910
Mock interviews.

115
00:05:48,590 --> 00:05:51,520
That sounds way less scary than facing a real interview panel.

116
00:05:52,260 --> 00:05:56,480
Are there any other resources that you recommend for improving speaking skills?

117
00:05:57,570 --> 00:06:00,070
Joining a group like Toastmasters, that can be amazing.

118
00:06:00,170 --> 00:06:06,760
It's a really supportive community where you can practice public speaking and get feedback from people who've been doing it for a while.

119
00:06:07,340 --> 00:06:11,280
It's a fantastic way to build confidence and meet other people who are on the same journey.

120
00:06:11,400 --> 00:06:12,000
Hmm.

121
00:06:12,020 --> 00:06:12,800
That's a really good idea.

122
00:06:13,500 --> 00:06:15,710
I think I might check out my local Jostmasters chapter.

123
00:06:16,310 --> 00:06:16,410
Yeah.

124
00:06:16,430 --> 00:06:17,870
So now what about listening?

125
00:06:18,150 --> 00:06:22,940
I feel like I zone out sometimes in meetings, especially when they start throwing around technical jargon.

126
00:06:23,480 --> 00:06:24,000
Ah, yes.

127
00:06:24,440 --> 00:06:25,360
Active listening.

128
00:06:25,400 --> 00:06:28,230
That's a crucial skill for any business setting.

129
00:06:28,550 --> 00:06:30,030
It's more than just hearing the words.

130
00:06:30,130 --> 00:06:32,920
It's about actually understanding what's being said.

131
00:06:33,020 --> 00:06:34,280
Okay, but how do we do that?

132
00:06:34,500 --> 00:06:36,060
Like, how do we become better listeners?

133
00:06:36,200 --> 00:06:39,070
So one technique that works really well is called reflecting.

134
00:06:39,570 --> 00:06:44,690
It's basically summarizing what you heard back to the speaker to make sure you got it right.

135
00:06:45,390 --> 00:06:49,120
For example, you might say, so what you're saying is, or...

136
00:06:49,100 --> 00:06:51,400
Just to make sure I'm on the same page, you mean.

137
00:06:51,720 --> 00:06:52,420
Ah, that makes sense.

138
00:06:52,700 --> 00:06:56,870
It shows them you're paying attention and actually trying to understand their point of view.

139
00:06:57,030 --> 00:06:57,530
Exactly.

140
00:06:57,590 --> 00:07:01,610
And it gives the speaker a chance to clarify anything that might have been misunderstood.

141
00:07:02,130 --> 00:07:06,460
It's a simple technique, but it can be really powerful in keeping the communication clear.

142
00:07:06,740 --> 00:07:08,000
Yeah, I could see how that would be helpful.

143
00:07:08,380 --> 00:07:13,350
It avoids those moments where things get lost in translation, which can happen in business sometimes.

144
00:07:13,730 --> 00:07:16,170
Are there any other active listening tips that you recommend?

145
00:07:16,390 --> 00:07:17,010
Definitely.

146
00:07:17,480 --> 00:07:19,880
Try to really focus on the speaker's main points.

147
00:07:20,450 --> 00:07:22,850
What's the core message they're trying to get across?

148
00:07:23,410 --> 00:07:25,930
Pay attention to their tone of voice and body language.

149
00:07:26,890 --> 00:07:33,020
Those nonverbal cues, they can actually give you valuable insights into what they're really trying to say and how they're feeling.

150
00:07:33,140 --> 00:07:38,610
So it's like reading between the lines and paying attention to those subtle things that can add to our understanding.

151
00:07:38,730 --> 00:07:39,430
Precisely.

152
00:07:39,470 --> 00:07:44,520
Active listening, it's a skill that takes practice, but the benefits are definitely worth it.

153
00:07:44,820 --> 00:07:48,830
It can transform your communication and make your professional relationships stronger.

154
00:07:49,590 --> 00:07:53,240
Okay, I'm feeling inspired to step up my listening game.

155
00:07:53,540 --> 00:08:02,580
But all this talk about active listening makes me wonder, how can we train our ears to better understand different accents and speaking styles?

156
00:08:02,700 --> 00:08:03,560
That's a great question.

157
00:08:03,620 --> 00:08:07,180
And it's a pretty common challenge, especially for people working in international business.

158
00:08:07,240 --> 00:08:07,840
Yeah, exactly.

159
00:08:07,860 --> 00:08:13,730
Sometimes I feel like I'm playing guess the accent in meetings, especially when the conversation starts going really fast.

160
00:08:13,750 --> 00:08:17,900
Well, imagine you're training for an international listening marathon.

161
00:08:18,680 --> 00:08:22,820
Immersing yourself in different audio content, that can really improve your comprehension.

162
00:08:23,120 --> 00:08:26,890
Like training my ears to be multilingual.

163
00:08:27,090 --> 00:08:27,610
Precisely.

164
00:08:28,150 --> 00:08:33,580
Listen to podcasts, watch movies, or even tune into news channels from different countries or regions.

165
00:08:34,300 --> 00:08:41,510
The more you expose yourself to different accents, the better your ear will become at picking up those nuances in pronunciation and intonation.

166
00:08:41,870 --> 00:08:42,130
Okay.

167
00:08:42,190 --> 00:08:46,750
I'm adding International Listening Marathon to my business English to-do list.

168
00:08:46,910 --> 00:08:47,200
Yeah.

169
00:08:47,240 --> 00:08:49,320
Sounds like a fun way to boost my listening skills.

170
00:08:49,420 --> 00:08:55,970
And remember, if you're in a conversation and you miss something, don't be afraid to politely ask the speaker to repeat themselves.

171
00:08:56,690 --> 00:09:01,210
Most people are happy to clarify, especially if it helps to make sure everyone's understanding each other.

172
00:09:01,230 --> 00:09:02,260
That's good to know.

173
00:09:02,940 --> 00:09:05,240
It reminds us that communication is a two-way street.

174
00:09:05,660 --> 00:09:08,750
We're all in this together, you know, trying to understand and be understood.

175
00:09:08,790 --> 00:09:09,670
Exactly.

176
00:09:09,690 --> 00:09:14,410
And speaking of understanding, we've covered vocabulary, writing, speaking, listening.

177
00:09:14,750 --> 00:09:18,580
That brings us to the final and maybe the trickiest part, cultural etiquette.

178
00:09:18,730 --> 00:09:19,470
Uh-oh, yeah.

179
00:09:19,910 --> 00:09:21,350
That one feels like a real challenge.

180
00:09:21,690 --> 00:09:24,430
There are so many different cultures and customs out there.

181
00:09:25,080 --> 00:09:26,820
Where do we even start to figure all that out?

182
00:09:27,120 --> 00:09:29,280
It begins with a change in how you think about things.

183
00:09:30,320 --> 00:09:38,350
Recognize that, well, your own cultural background, it shapes how you communicate, and other cultures might have totally different norms and expectations.

184
00:09:38,470 --> 00:09:46,220
So step one is knowing that my way isn't the only way and that I need to be open to different approaches.

185
00:09:46,270 --> 00:09:47,130
Exactly.

186
00:09:47,150 --> 00:09:51,720
It's all about developing a sense of curiosity and respect for other perspectives.

187
00:09:52,300 --> 00:09:53,800
And research is really important, too.

188
00:09:54,160 --> 00:09:59,230
Before you interact with people from another culture, take some time to learn about their communication customs.

189
00:09:59,250 --> 00:10:03,630
You can find tons of resources online, books, even cultural training programs.

190
00:10:03,820 --> 00:10:04,200
Oh, okay.

191
00:10:04,220 --> 00:10:06,800
So I can do my homework and be prepared for those interactions.

192
00:10:07,140 --> 00:10:09,100
What kind of specific things should I be researching?

193
00:10:09,220 --> 00:10:16,330
Pay attention to things like tone of voice, how formal they are, appropriate topics for conversation, even body language.

194
00:10:16,750 --> 00:10:22,830
Like in some cultures, interrupting is considered rude, but in others, it actually shows that you're really engaged.

195
00:10:23,670 --> 00:10:28,240
And what might seem like a friendly gesture in one culture could be offensive in another.

196
00:10:28,520 --> 00:10:32,750
It's amazing how much meaning can be packed into those little nonverbal cues.

197
00:10:32,810 --> 00:10:33,050
Yeah.

198
00:10:33,510 --> 00:10:37,100
I'm really starting to understand why cultural etiquette is so important in business English.

199
00:10:37,380 --> 00:10:37,760
It is.

200
00:10:37,960 --> 00:10:41,380
And, you know, there are often subtle nuances even within culture.

201
00:10:41,500 --> 00:10:46,330
So it's important to just be observant and try to pick up on those cues from the people you're talking to.

202
00:10:46,430 --> 00:10:52,220
So it's about being present, paying attention and adapting how we approach things based on the situation.

203
00:10:52,520 --> 00:10:53,040
Exactly.

204
00:10:53,140 --> 00:10:54,500
It's a constant learning process.

205
00:10:55,110 --> 00:10:56,190
Be patient with yourself.

206
00:10:56,330 --> 00:10:58,250
You'll make mistakes along the way, but that's OK.

207
00:10:58,750 --> 00:11:02,720
The important thing is to learn from them and, you know, refine how you approach things.

208
00:11:02,980 --> 00:11:04,200
This has been so insightful.

209
00:11:04,280 --> 00:11:07,170
It's like I'm already seeing business communication in a whole new way.

210
00:11:07,180 --> 00:11:08,050
That's great to hear.

211
00:11:08,110 --> 00:11:12,900
Remember, mastering business English, it's a journey, not a destination.

212
00:11:12,940 --> 00:11:14,840
It's not about being perfect.

213
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:17,630
It's about always trying to improve and adapt.

214
00:11:17,690 --> 00:11:17,910
Right.

215
00:11:18,490 --> 00:11:20,430
Progress, not perfection.

216
00:11:20,550 --> 00:11:21,310
Exactly.

217
00:11:21,330 --> 00:11:29,010
And speaking of progress, we've covered a lot today laying the groundwork for mastering business English, but we've only just begun.

218
00:11:29,070 --> 00:11:37,220
Stay tuned for part two, where we'll dive even deeper into practical strategies to help you boost your skills and really achieve your communication goals.

219
00:11:37,620 --> 00:11:39,150
Wow, this is just the beginning.

220
00:11:39,190 --> 00:11:41,610
I'm excited to keep learning and see where this journey takes us.

221
00:11:42,380 --> 00:11:43,670
Welcome back to the Deep Dive.

222
00:11:44,170 --> 00:11:49,470
Last time we talked about how important business English is, especially in today's global business world.

223
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:56,940
We covered the key areas you need to really master, things like vocabulary, writing, speaking, listening, and of course, cultural etiquette.

224
00:11:57,100 --> 00:11:58,890
Yeah, I feel like we already learned so much.

225
00:11:58,910 --> 00:12:06,740
I'm ready to dig a little deeper into the practical stuff, the strategies that can actually help me use these ideas in real life situations.

226
00:12:06,960 --> 00:12:07,480
Let's do it.

227
00:12:07,580 --> 00:12:12,220
Remember how we talked about immersing yourself in industry-specific content to build your vocabulary?

228
00:12:12,510 --> 00:12:12,770
Yeah.

229
00:12:13,210 --> 00:12:16,790
Reading journals and articles in my field instead of just memorizing words from a dictionary.

230
00:12:17,450 --> 00:12:20,440
It's like learning a new language by immersing yourself in it.

231
00:12:20,600 --> 00:12:21,240
Exactly.

232
00:12:21,260 --> 00:12:23,880
It's about absorbing the language naturally.

233
00:12:23,900 --> 00:12:25,800
And don't just passively read.

234
00:12:26,040 --> 00:12:27,750
Really engage with the material.

235
00:12:27,890 --> 00:12:34,290
Underlying keywords, write down any questions you have, maybe even summarize paragraphs in your own words.

236
00:12:34,770 --> 00:12:36,880
This will help you make that vocabulary stick.

237
00:12:37,140 --> 00:12:37,930
That's a great tip.

238
00:12:37,950 --> 00:12:39,630
It makes reading way more interactive.

